{
  "summary": "Exposes `interfaces::Chain` over IPC by adding Cap'n Proto schemas and proxy wrappers, split into a stable `Chain` interface and an `UnstableChain` interface. The stable interface covers basic block lookups for external clients, while `UnstableChain` exposes the remaining methods for Bitcoin Core's own internal clients. It also updates the `src/ipc/libmultiprocess` subtree and makes minor adjustments to `interfaces::Chain` method signatures for proxy compatibility.",
  "problem": "Processes connecting to `bitcoin-node` over IPC cannot access chain data or subscribe to chain events, blocking out-of-process wallet separation (#10102). Exposing the entire internal `interfaces::Chain` interface directly to third parties would freeze internal wallet APIs against future refactoring.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: \"Processes connected to `bitcoin-node` over IPC (with `-ipcbind`) can use the `Mining` interface, but they can't currently use the `Chain` interface read chain data or follow chain events. Chain data could be useful to third-party clients and Bitcoin Core's own wallet could use this as well to run in a separate process (#10102).\"",
    "PR description citing #34981: \"But exposing all of `interfaces::Chain` to external clients would make its methods hard to change, since the wallet uses them as internal API (#34981).\""
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#36520"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#10102",
      "#19460",
      "#19461"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Carries a large `src/ipc/libmultiprocess` subtree update to version 14 along with substantial Cap'n Proto schema and proxy generation code.",
  "changed_since_previous": "The interface is now split into a stable Cap'n Proto `Chain` interface and an `UnstableChain` interface rather than exposing the whole interface identically; the libmultiprocess subtree update is refreshed to v14; and an explicit dependency on crash fix #36520 is noted.",
  "card": "Exposes the Chain interface over IPC using Cap'n Proto, splitting it into a version-stable Chain interface with basic block lookup methods and an UnstableChain interface for Bitcoin Core internal consumers. Currently, chain data and notifications are inaccessible over IPC, blocking multiprocess wallet separation (#10102) and external clients connecting via -ipcbind. This is a core prerequisite for running the wallet in an isolated process (#19460) and depends on #36520.",
